\section{Finite-Step Regional Capture of the Live MEKF}
\label{sec:finite-live-capture}

The block-local ISS result of Sec.~\ref{sec:iss-block-basin} proves that the
normal Live recursion possesses a local invariant ISS neighborhood.  It is not,
however, used below as an operational startup bottleneck.  The deployed handoff
can be appreciably farther from the equilibrium and may undergo a short
covariance/OU settling transient before contraction becomes dominant.  The
operational statement is therefore proved directly for the complete nonlinear
Live map: a structured post-handoff set is propagated by verified finite-step
comparison maps until it enters a smaller robustly invariant regional set.  No
individual sample, and no individual lifted interval, is required to contract.

\subsection{Structured regional coordinates and the exact lifted map}

Use the sensitive/kinematic split of
Eqs.~\eqref{eq:iss-block-xi}--\eqref{eq:iss-block-ell},
\begin{equation}
 \vct e=(\vct e_\xi,\vct e_\ell),
 \qquad
 \vct e_\xi=(\delta\vct\theta,\delta\vct b_g,
              \delta\vct S,\delta\vct a_w,\delta\vct b_a),
 \qquad
 \vct e_\ell=(\delta\vct v,\delta\vct p).
\end{equation}
Let $\pi_k$ denote the complete normal-Live proof mode at sample $k$: the
implemented covariance, enabled-state flags, staged OU/$R_S$ parameters, and
the accepted-update pattern known to the filter.  Over the declared compact
regional mode family choose uniformly positive-definite block metrics
$\mat Q_\xi(\pi)$ and $\mat Q_\ell(\pi)$ and define
\begin{align}
 r_{\xi,k}(\vct e)
 &:=\bigl(\vct e_\xi^\top\mat Q_\xi(\pi_k)\vct e_\xi\bigr)^{1/2},\\
 r_{\ell,k}(\vct e)
 &:=\bigl(\vct e_\ell^\top\mat Q_\ell(\pi_k)\vct e_\ell\bigr)^{1/2},\\
 \vct r_k(\vct e)&:=(r_{\xi,k},r_{\ell,k})^\top .
 \label{eq:capture-structured-radii}
\end{align}
The fixed dimensionless scaling used in Sec.~\ref{sec:iss-live-certificate} is
one admissible choice.  A mode-dependent metric derived from the implemented
covariance is another.  The scalar Riccati norm retained as a useful diagnostic
is
\begin{equation}
 \|\vct e\|_{P,k}:=
 \left(\vct e^\top\mat P_k^{-1}\vct e\right)^{1/2}.
 \label{eq:capture-riccati-norm}
\end{equation}
Uniform positive definiteness on the compact mode family makes these metrics
uniformly norm equivalent; the structured formulation is used because it does
not force the admissible attitude/$S$/bias errors and the kinematic errors into
one spherical radius.

For a lift of $m\ge1$ samples, denote the \emph{complete implemented nonlinear
error map} by
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \vct e_{k+m}
 =\mathcal F_{k,m}
   (\vct e_k,\vct d_{k:k+m-1};\pi_{k:k+m}).}
 \label{eq:capture-exact-lift}
\end{equation}
This map contains the exact prediction, the executed accelerometer and
magnetometer corrections, the full $S=0$ Kalman correction, quaternion
injection/reset, covariance propagation, state-enable staging, and the
predictable adaptive schedule.  In particular, the $S=0$ update retains its
attitude and bias rows through cross-covariance.  No Schmidt, block-only, or
gain-truncated approximation is introduced for the proof.

For $\vct a\in\mathbb R_+^2$, write
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal C_k(\vct a)
 :=\{\vct e:\vct r_k(\vct e)\preceq\vct a\},
 \label{eq:capture-structured-box}
\end{equation}
where inequalities between two-vectors are componentwise.  Fix positive vectors
\begin{equation}
 0\prec\vct R_B\prec\vct R_C\preceq\vct R_D .
 \label{eq:capture-region-order}
\end{equation}
Here $\mathcal C_k(\vct R_D)$ is the chart/measurement-safe proof domain,
$\mathcal C_k(\vct R_C)$ is the certified outer handoff/capture set, and
$\mathcal B_k:=\mathcal C_k(\vct R_B)$ is the inner regional set.  The proof
allows the state to grow beyond $\vct R_C$ temporarily, provided every verified
prefix remains inside $\vct R_D$.

\subsection{Exact comparison envelopes}

For a disturbance budget $\|\vct d\|_\infty\le\overline d$ define the
componentwise comparison envelope of the complete lift by
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 [\mathcal T_{k,m}(\vct a;\overline d)]_i
 :=\sup
 \left\{
 r_{i,k+m}\!\left(
 \mathcal F_{k,m}(\vct e,\vct d;\pi)
 \right):
 \vct e\in\mathcal C_k(\vct a),
 \ \|\vct d\|_\infty\le\overline d,
 \ \pi\in\Pi_{k,m}
 \right\},}
 \quad i\in\{\xi,\ell\}.
 \label{eq:capture-comparison-envelope}
\end{equation}
The admissible set $\Pi_{k,m}$ contains only predictable schedules and accepted
update patterns satisfying the declared Live assumptions.  Compactness and
continuity make the suprema finite.  Because
$\mathcal C_k(\vct a)\subseteq\mathcal C_k(\vct b)$ whenever
$\vct a\preceq\vct b$, the map $\mathcal T_{k,m}$ is monotone:
\begin{equation}
 \vct a\preceq\vct b
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
 \mathcal T_{k,m}(\vct a;\overline d)
 \preceq
 \mathcal T_{k,m}(\vct b;\overline d).
 \label{eq:capture-comparison-monotone}
\end{equation}
Prefix envelopes $\mathcal T_{k,j}^{\rm pre}$, $1\le j<m$, are defined by the
same supremum with $\mathcal F_{k,j}$.  Equation~\eqref{eq:capture-comparison-envelope}
is deliberately taken on the complete nonlinear map; it therefore preserves
any cancellation produced by the Kalman correction, quaternion reset, and
subsequent prediction instead of applying a triangle inequality separately to
linear and nonlinear pieces.

Choose an admissible sequence of lift lengths $m_n\ge1$ and let $k_0=k_H$,
$k_{n+1}=k_n+m_n$.  Define
\begin{equation}
 \overline{\vct r}_{n+1}
 =\mathcal T_{k_n,m_n}(\overline{\vct r}_n;\overline d).
 \label{eq:capture-comparison-sequence}
\end{equation}
The maps may change with $n$; hence the first post-handoff lifts may expand and
later lifts may contract more strongly.  No frozen covariance and no common
one-step contraction factor are assumed.

\begin{theorem}[Finite-step regional Live capture and practical ISS]
\label{thm:finite-live-capture}
Assume the exact comparison envelopes above are valid on
$\mathcal C_k(\vct R_D)$.  Starting from
$\overline{\vct r}^{\,C}_0=\vct R_C$, generate
Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-comparison-sequence}.  Suppose there is a finite
$N_C$ such that
\begin{align}
 \overline{\vct r}^{\,C}_n&\preceq\vct R_D,
 &&0\le n\le N_C,\label{eq:capture-lift-domain}\\
 \mathcal T^{\rm pre}_{k_n,j}
 (\overline{\vct r}^{\,C}_n;\overline d)
 &\preceq\vct R_D,
 &&0\le n<N_C,\quad1\le j<m_n,
 \label{eq:capture-prefix-domain}\\
 \overline{\vct r}^{\,C}_{N_C}&\preceq\vct R_B.
 \label{eq:capture-finite-entry}
\end{align}
Assume further that every admissible post-capture normal-Live lift satisfies the
robust invariance condition
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathcal T_{k,m}(\vct R_B;\overline d)\preceq\vct R_B,}
 \label{eq:capture-inner-invariance}
\end{equation}
with chart-safe prefixes, and that for some positive weight vector $\vct w$,
$0<\lambda_B<1$, and class-$\mathcal K$ function $\gamma_B$,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \|\mathcal T_{k,m}(\vct a;\overline d)\|_{\vct w,\infty}
 \le
 \lambda_B\|\vct a\|_{\vct w,\infty}
 +\gamma_B(\overline d),
 \qquad
 0\preceq\vct a\preceq\vct R_B,}
 \label{eq:capture-inner-contraction}
\end{equation}
where
$\|\vct a\|_{\vct w,\infty}:=\max_i a_i/w_i$.
Then every deterministic Live trajectory beginning in
$\mathcal C_{k_H}(\vct R_C)$ remains in the certified chart/measurement domain,
enters $\mathcal B_{k_{N_C}}$ after at most $N_C$ lifted intervals, and remains
in the inner regional set thereafter.  After capture,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \|\vct r_{k_{N_C+j}}\|_{\vct w,\infty}
 \le
 \lambda_B^j\|\vct r_{k_{N_C}}\|_{\vct w,\infty}
 +\frac{1-\lambda_B^j}{1-\lambda_B}\gamma_B(\overline d).}
 \label{eq:capture-practical-iss}
\end{equation}
Thus the full nonlinear Live recursion is regionally practically ISS on the
captured set.  For $\overline d=0$, the captured error converges exponentially
to zero.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let an arbitrary initial error satisfy
$\vct r_{k_H}(\vct e_{k_H})\preceq\vct R_C
=\overline{\vct r}^{\,C}_0$.  Monotonicity
Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-comparison-monotone} and the definition of the exact
envelope give by induction
\begin{equation}
 \vct r_{k_n}(\vct e_{k_n})
 \preceq\overline{\vct r}^{\,C}_n,
 \qquad0\le n\le N_C.
\end{equation}
The prefix inequalities
Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-prefix-domain} therefore keep every intermediate sample
inside $\mathcal C(\vct R_D)$, even when a prefix or a complete early lift grows.
Equation~\eqref{eq:capture-finite-entry} then yields
$\vct r_{k_{N_C}}\preceq\vct R_B$.  Applying monotonicity once more to
Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-inner-invariance} proves forward invariance of the inner
set.  Finally Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-inner-contraction} applied recursively after
capture gives the geometric-series bound
Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-practical-iss}.  No inclusion in the separate block-local
entrance condition is required.
\end{proof}

The theorem is a finite-step Lyapunov/comparison result rather than a one-step
contraction theorem.  A composite sequence
$\mathcal T_{k_{q-1},m_{q-1}}\circ\cdots\circ
 \mathcal T_{k_0,m_0}$ may map the outer set strictly inward even when one or
more constituent maps expand it.  This is the proof counterpart of the observed
post-\texttt{goLive()} ringing.

\subsection{Handoff-specific capture time}

The basin-wide bound above starts from the complete outer set.  Startup normally
starts from a much smaller structured handoff envelope.  Let
$\vct R_H\preceq\vct R_C$ be any certified handoff bound and define
\begin{equation}
 \overline{\vct r}^{\,H}_0=\vct R_H,
 \qquad
 \overline{\vct r}^{\,H}_{n+1}
 =\mathcal T_{k_n,m_n}
  (\overline{\vct r}^{\,H}_n;\overline d).
 \label{eq:capture-handoff-sequence}
\end{equation}
The rigorous startup capture count is
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 N_H:=\min\{n\ge0:
 \overline{\vct r}^{\,H}_n\preceq\vct R_B\}.}
 \label{eq:capture-count}
\end{equation}
Whenever the outer-set certificate exists, monotonicity gives
$N_H\le N_C$.  The corresponding time bounds are
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 T_H\le h_{\max}\sum_{n=0}^{N_H-1}m_n,
 \qquad
 T_C\le h_{\max}\sum_{n=0}^{N_C-1}m_n.}
 \label{eq:capture-time}
\end{equation}
Thus the startup claim is not penalized by pretending every accepted Mahony
handoff begins on the extreme boundary of the outer capture set.

\subsection{Scalar quadratic bound as an optional sufficient corollary}

The earlier split bound remains a valid but generally more conservative way to
construct an envelope.  If, in a scalar norm, verified constants satisfy
\begin{align}
 \|\mat L_{k+m}^{-1}\mat\Psi_{k,m}\mat L_k\|_2
 &\le\chi_C,\label{eq:capture-linear-contraction}\\
 \|\mat L_{k+m}^{-1}\vct\varphi_{k,m}(\vct e)\|_2
 &\le c_C\|\vct e\|_k^2,
 \label{eq:capture-nonlinear-bound}\\
 \|\mat L_{k+m}^{-1}\vct\Delta_{k,m}(\vct e,\vct d)\|_2
 &\le g_C\overline d,
 \label{eq:capture-disturbance-bound}
\end{align}
then
\begin{equation}
 r_{n+1}\le
 \psi_C(r_n):=\chi_Cr_n+c_Cr_n^2+g_C\overline d.
 \label{eq:capture-scalar-recursion}
\end{equation}
When
\begin{equation}
 \Delta_C=(1-\chi_C)^2-4c_Cg_C\overline d>0,
 \qquad
 R_\pm=\frac{(1-\chi_C)\pm\sqrt{\Delta_C}}{2c_C},
 \label{eq:capture-roots}
\end{equation}
$\psi_C(r)<r$ on $(R_-,R_+)$.  Even in this scalar special case the sharp
finite certificate is obtained by iterating $\psi_C$ from the actual handoff
bound, not by dividing the annulus width by its single worst decrement.  The
quadratic construction is therefore a sufficient corollary of the exact-map
framework, not the operational theorem itself.

\subsection{Operational capture-certificate contract}
\label{sec:capture-certificate-contract}

The numerical certificate must start from the covariance and estimator mode
produced by the actual \texttt{goLive()} handoff, including staged OU
parameters, covariance synchronization, the enabled linear block, the full
$S=0$ gain, and the initially held accelerometer-bias update.  It must not insert
the \SI{120}{s} equilibrium covariance warm-up used by the separate
steady-Live diagnostic.

Let $\vct R_H$ be the structured worst-case handoff radius supplied by
Sec.~\ref{sec:semiglobal-handoff}.  A useful dimensionless handoff margin is
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \eta_H:=
 \min_{i\in\{\xi,\ell\}}
 \left(1-\frac{R_{H,i}}{R_{C,i}}\right).}
 \label{eq:capture-handoff-margin}
\end{equation}
The certificate reports at least
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \{\vct R_H,\vct R_B,\vct R_C,\vct R_D,
   \eta_H,N_H,T_H,N_C,T_C,B_{\rm pre},
   \vct w,\lambda_B,\gamma_B\},}
 \label{eq:capture-certificate-outputs}
\end{equation}
where $B_{\rm pre}$ summarizes the largest verified normalized prefix
amplification and $N_C,T_C$ are optional when only the declared handoff family,
rather than the whole outer box, is being certified.  The operational startup
pass conditions are
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \vct R_H\prec\vct R_C\preceq\vct R_D,
 \qquad
 \overline{\vct r}^{\,H}_{N_H}\preceq\vct R_B,
 \qquad
 N_H<\infty,
 \qquad
 \lambda_B<1,}
 \label{eq:capture-certificate-pass}
\end{equation}
together with the prefix-domain and inner-invariance conditions
Eqs.~\eqref{eq:capture-prefix-domain} and
\eqref{eq:capture-inner-invariance}.  No prescribed ratio between the outer and
inner radii is a stability condition.  The componentwise ratios
$R_{C,i}/R_{B,i}$ and whether the handoff family already intersects or lies
outside $\mathcal B$ may be reported descriptively, but they are not used to
manufacture a pass.

\subsection{Stochastic Live qualification}
\label{sec:capture-stochastic}

The deterministic capture theorem uses $\overline d$ only for disturbances that
are legitimately bounded on the declared interval, such as bounded model
mismatch or a prescribed deterministic perturbation.  Gaussian process and
measurement noise are treated separately because their infinite-horizon sample
supremum is almost surely not bounded by a fixed deterministic constant.

\begin{theorem}[Mean-square practical stability under stochastic Live noise]
\label{thm:capture-stochastic-live}
Let $\mathcal F_n$ be the filtration at the post-capture lifted indices and let
$V_n\ge0$ be a Lyapunov function uniformly equivalent to the squared structured
norm: for constants $0<a_1\le a_2<\infty$,
\begin{equation}
 a_1\|\vct r_n\|_{\vct w,\infty}^2
 \le V_n\le
 a_2\|\vct r_n\|_{\vct w,\infty}^2.
 \label{eq:capture-stochastic-equivalence}
\end{equation}
Suppose that, for every lifted index on the horizon under consideration, the
exact stochastic Live map satisfies almost surely the conditional drift bound
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathbb E[V_{n+1}\mid\mathcal F_n]
 \le\lambda_sV_n+\sigma_s^2,
 \qquad 0<\lambda_s<1.}
 \label{eq:capture-stochastic-drift}
\end{equation}
If this condition holds for all $n\ge0$, then
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathbb E[V_n]
 \le
 \lambda_s^n\mathbb E[V_0]
 +\frac{1-\lambda_s^n}{1-\lambda_s}\sigma_s^2,}
 \label{eq:capture-stochastic-ms}
\end{equation}
and consequently
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E\|\vct r_n\|_{\vct w,\infty}^2
 \le\frac{1}{a_1}
 \left(
 \lambda_s^n\mathbb E[V_0]
 +\frac{\sigma_s^2}{1-\lambda_s}
 \right).
 \label{eq:capture-stochastic-state-ms}
\end{equation}
For every $A>0$,
\begin{equation}
 \Pr\{V_n\ge A\}
 \le\frac{1}{A}
 \left(
 \lambda_s^n\mathbb E[V_0]
 +\frac{1-\lambda_s^n}{1-\lambda_s}\sigma_s^2
 \right),
 \label{eq:capture-stochastic-one-time}
\end{equation}
and, over any finite horizon $0\le n\le N$ on which
Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-stochastic-drift} is valid,
\begin{equation}
 \Pr\left\{\max_{0\le j\le N}V_j\ge A\right\}
 \le\frac{1}{A}\sum_{j=0}^{N}
 \left(
 \lambda_s^j\mathbb E[V_0]
 +\frac{1-\lambda_s^j}{1-\lambda_s}\sigma_s^2
 \right).
 \label{eq:capture-stochastic-finite-hp}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Taking total expectation in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-stochastic-drift} and iterating the scalar affine
recursion gives Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-stochastic-ms};
Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-stochastic-state-ms} follows from the lower norm-equivalence
bound.  Markov's inequality gives
Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-stochastic-one-time}.  Applying that inequality at each
$j=0,\ldots,N$ and using the union bound gives
Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-stochastic-finite-hp}.
\end{proof}

The stochastic theorem does not assert almost-sure invariance of a finite local
chart under unbounded Gaussian support.  Its infinite-horizon mean-square claim
requires Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-stochastic-drift} for all lifted indices; when the
drift inequality is certified only on a finite stochastic domain or horizon,
only the corresponding finite-horizon bounds are claimed.  Thus the
deterministic finite-capture statement and the stochastic steady-Live statement
have distinct disturbance hypotheses.